And it just stayed with me long enough that I thought, you know, by the way, I was, I think I was in my second or third year as an assistant professor by this time, and I'm making no progress on anything. I'm not, I don't have a, you know, I'm doing stuff, but it wasn't like there was anything I was identified with that was going anywhere. So I thought, you know, obviously I'm interested in memory, implicit memory is clearly interesting. Let me start by replicating the Jacobi experiment. Maizuri noticed that all the names that Larry Jacobi had used in his experiment were male names.
